<div class="tbeg">
  <div class="tbeg-wrap">
    <p class="tbeg-eyebrow">DTF guide</p>
    <h1>What is DTF printing?</h1>
    <p class="tbeg-lead">DTF stands for direct to film. It's how we put a full color design on a hat without thread. If your logo has gradients, a photo, lots of colors or fine detail, or it came out of an AI tool, DTF prints it the way it looks on your screen.</p>
    <ul class="tbeg-jump">
      <li><a href="#how">How it works</a></li>
      <li><a href="#durability">Durability &amp; care</a></li>
      <li><a href="#compare">DTF vs embroidery</a></li>
      <li><a href="#designs">Best designs</a></li>
      <li><a href="#ai">AI designs</a></li>
      <li><a href="#faq">FAQ</a></li>
    </ul>

    <section id="how">
      <h2>How DTF works</h2>
      <p>DTF is a heat transfer, but it isn't vinyl and it isn't a sticker. Your design is printed with real ink, then bonded into the fabric with heat and pressure.</p>
      <div class="tbdg-steps">
        <div class="tbdg-step"><svg viewBox="0 0 160 110" aria-hidden="true"><rect x="0" y="0" width="160" height="110" rx="12" fill="#F4F1FB"/><rect x="30" y="18" width="100" height="62" rx="4" fill="#fff" stroke="#C9C2DE" stroke-width="2"/><rect x="40" y="10" width="80" height="12" rx="3" fill="#14121C"/><circle cx="62" cy="49" r="13" fill="#E8435A"/><circle cx="80" cy="49" r="13" fill="#F2B632" opacity=".9"/><circle cx="98" cy="49" r="13" fill="#2C8FD6" opacity=".9"/><rect x="52" y="86" width="56" height="6" rx="3" fill="#C9C2DE"/></svg><div><b><span>1</span>Print</b><p>Your design is printed in full color onto a clear film, with a layer of white ink behind it so colors stay bright on dark hats.</p></div></div>
        <div class="tbdg-step"><svg viewBox="0 0 160 110" aria-hidden="true"><rect x="0" y="0" width="160" height="110" rx="12" fill="#F4F1FB"/><rect x="30" y="42" width="100" height="44" rx="4" fill="#fff" stroke="#C9C2DE" stroke-width="2"/><circle cx="62" cy="64" r="11" fill="#E8435A"/><circle cx="80" cy="64" r="11" fill="#F2B632"/><circle cx="98" cy="64" r="11" fill="#2C8FD6"/><circle cx="50" cy="20" r="1.8" fill="#9A93B3"/><circle cx="62" cy="28" r="1.8" fill="#9A93B3"/><circle cx="74" cy="16" r="1.8" fill="#9A93B3"/><circle cx="86" cy="30" r="1.8" fill="#9A93B3"/><circle cx="98" cy="22" r="1.8" fill="#9A93B3"/><circle cx="110" cy="34" r="1.8" fill="#9A93B3"/><circle cx="70" cy="38" r="1.8" fill="#9A93B3"/><circle cx="92" cy="40" r="1.8" fill="#9A93B3"/><circle cx="56" cy="36" r="1.8" fill="#9A93B3"/><circle cx="104" cy="14" r="1.8" fill="#9A93B3"/><path d="M64 6h32l-6 10H70z" fill="#5028AA"/></svg><div><b><span>2</span>Powder</b><p>A fine adhesive powder goes on while the ink is wet, then it's cured with heat. That powder is what bonds the print to the hat.</p></div></div>
        <div class="tbdg-step"><svg viewBox="0 0 160 110" aria-hidden="true"><rect x="0" y="0" width="160" height="110" rx="12" fill="#F4F1FB"/><path d="M28 82c0-30 22-54 52-54s52 24 52 54z" fill="#3B3F4A"/><path d="M20 82h120c-6 10-30 14-60 14S26 92 20 82z" fill="#2B2E36"/><rect x="38" y="8" width="84" height="16" rx="4" fill="#5028AA"/><rect x="74" y="24" width="12" height="10" fill="#5028AA"/><path d="M58 54c0-6 10-10 22-10s22 4 22 10v8H58z" fill="#fff" opacity=".9"/></svg><div><b><span>3</span>Press</b><p>We heat press the transfer onto your hat in our shop in Burlington, NC.</p></div></div>
        <div class="tbdg-step"><svg viewBox="0 0 160 110" aria-hidden="true"><rect x="0" y="0" width="160" height="110" rx="12" fill="#F4F1FB"/><path d="M28 84c0-30 22-54 52-54s52 24 52 54z" fill="#3B3F4A"/><path d="M20 84h120c-6 10-30 14-60 14S26 94 20 84z" fill="#2B2E36"/><circle cx="68" cy="60" r="9" fill="#E8435A"/><circle cx="80" cy="60" r="9" fill="#F2B632"/><circle cx="92" cy="60" r="9" fill="#2C8FD6"/><path d="M104 22l22-10 4 10-22 10z" fill="#fff" stroke="#C9C2DE" stroke-width="2"/></svg><div><b><span>4</span>Peel</b><p>The film peels away and only your design is left, bonded to the fabric with a thin, smooth finish.</p></div></div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="durability">
      <h2>How long does DTF last?</h2>
      <p>A properly pressed DTF print is bonded into the fibers, so it flexes with the hat instead of sitting on top of it. On shirts, DTF transfers are commonly rated for 50 or more washes. Hats rarely go through a washing machine, so for most people the print lasts as long as the hat does.</p>
      <p>Embroidery is still the toughest decoration there is. After years of heavy sun and sweat, any print can start to show some fading or wear at the edges. If you want a hat that outlasts everything, go with thread. If you want full color at a lower price, DTF holds up great.</p>
      <div class="tbdg-care">
        <div class="tbeg-card tbeg-ok"><h3>Do</h3><ul style="margin:6px 0 4px">
          <li>Spot clean with cool water and mild soap</li>
          <li>Hand wash cold if the whole hat needs it</li>
          <li>Let it air dry, ideally on something round so it keeps its shape</li>
          <li>Wait 24 hours after you get it before the first wash</li>
        </ul></div>
        <div class="tbeg-card tbeg-no"><h3>Don't</h3><ul style="margin:6px 0 4px">
          <li>Put it in the dryer or dishwasher</li>
          <li>Use bleach or harsh cleaners on the print</li>
          <li>Scrub the print with a stiff brush</li>
          <li>Iron over the print</li>
        </ul></div>
      </div>
    </section>

