      <p style="font-size:15px;color:var(--muted)">Not sure which one fits your logo? <a href="sms:+13365856176">Text us</a> or <a href="mailto:help@threadbreak.com">email it over</a> and we'll tell you what we'd pick.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="designs">
      <h2>What prints best</h2>
      <div class="tbeg-grid">
        <div class="tbeg-card tbeg-ok"><h3>Full color logos</h3><p>As many colors as you want, with no extra charge for more colors.</p></div>
        <div class="tbeg-card tbeg-ok"><h3>Gradients, glows and shadows</h3><p>The things thread can't do, DTF prints smooth.</p></div>
        <div class="tbeg-card tbeg-ok"><h3>Photos and realistic art</h3><p>Pets, people, product shots and detailed illustrations all print well.</p></div>
        <div class="tbeg-card tbeg-ok"><h3>Small text and thin lines</h3><p>Detail that would blur in thread stays sharp in print.</p></div>
      </div>
      <h2 style="margin-top:26px">Where DTF isn't the right fit</h2>
      <div class="tbeg-grid">
        <div class="tbeg-card tbeg-no"><h3>Mesh backs</h3><p>The print can't press flat on mesh, so side and back prints aren't offered on trucker hats. Fronts are fine.</p></div>
        <div class="tbeg-card tbeg-no"><h3>Very textured fabric</h3><p>Ridges like corduroy can show through the print. We'll point it out on your mockup.</p></div>
        <div class="tbeg-card tbeg-no"><h3>Oversized designs</h3><p>Front prints top out around 4.5 in wide by 2.5 in tall. Low profile hats, sides and back take a smaller print.</p></div>
        <div class="tbeg-card tbeg-no"><h3>That raised, stitched look</h3><p>DTF is flat. If you want texture and depth, go with <a href="/pages/custom-order">embroidery</a> or <a href="/pages/custom-patch-hats">a patch</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="ai">
      <h2>Got an AI-generated design? Print it.</h2>
      <div class="tbeg-note">
        <p>AI images are full of shading, glows and fine detail. That's tough to stitch in thread, but it's exactly what DTF is good at. Most AI designs print as they are, with no redrawing.</p>
        <p><strong>A few tips so it prints clean:</strong></p>
        <ul>
          <li>Download the biggest version you can, ideally 2000 pixels wide or more.</li>
          <li>Ask the AI tool for a <strong>transparent or plain background</strong>, or tell us to remove it.</li>
          <li><strong>Check every word.</strong> AI tools often warp letters or make up words, and we print exactly what's in the file.</li>
          <li>Skip tiny text along the edges. It's the first thing that gets lost at hat size.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>We check every file before printing and will let you know if it's too low resolution to print clean. Please make sure you have the rights to use the design.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="faq" class="tbdg-faq">
      <h2>DTF questions</h2>
      <details><summary>Will it crack or peel?</summary><p>Not with normal wear. DTF is flexible and bonds into the fabric, so it bends with the hat. Most cracking and peeling comes from dryer heat, harsh chemicals or scrubbing, so follow the care tips above.</p></details>
      <details><summary>Is DTF the same as vinyl?</summary><p>No. Vinyl is cut from a sheet of solid color and layered on, which gets thick and limits you to a few colors. DTF is printed ink, so it's thinner and can be any number of colors.</p></details>
      <details><summary>Does it work on dark hats?</summary><p>Yes. Every print has a white layer underneath, so colors stay bright on black, navy and camo.</p></details>
      <details><summary>What does it feel like?</summary><p>Thin and smooth with a slight sheen. You can feel it, but it's much lower than embroidery or a patch.</p></details>
      <details><summary>What's the minimum and lead time?</summary><p>5 hats minimum, same as our other custom hats. Current lead time is 2-4 weeks after you approve your free mockup.</p></details>
    </section>

